  {
   "section": "8.1",
   "title": "Introduction to prepositions and prepositional phrases",
   "pdf_pages": "16-17",
   "rule": "A preposition stands immediately before a noun phrase to show how it relates to another phrase. In Greek, a noun phrase (including a strong personal pronoun) after one of the basic prepositions is in the accusative. Only a few prepositions take the genitive: εναντίον, εξαιτίας, μεταξύ (the only ones that can be followed by weak personal pronouns); κατά can take either. A further group of (mostly place) adverbs may take either a genitive weak pronoun or a basic preposition + accusative (§8.3). A few prepositions can be followed by an adverb of place or time.",
